# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation Report

## Summary
Fixed the import organization in the React application to properly configure Vercel Web Analytics. The analytics package was already installed and configured, but had an import order issue that has been corrected.

## What Was Done

### 1. Verified Existing Installation
- Confirmed `@vercel/analytics` package (v2.0.1) was already installed in package.json
- Verified the `<Analytics />` component was already added to the App component
- Identified an import order issue where `useEffect` was imported at the end of the file

### 2. Fixed Import Order
**Modified:** `frontend/src/App.tsx`
- Moved `import { useEffect } from "react";` from the end of the file to the top
- Ensured proper React import conventions (React imports first, then third-party, then local imports)

### 3. Verification Steps Completed
✅ Dependencies installed successfully with `npm install`
✅ Build completed successfully with `npm run build`
✅ Linting passed with no errors using `npm run lint`
✅ No tests to run (no test script configured)

## Implementation Details

According to the latest Vercel documentation (fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), the correct implementation for React/Vite projects is:

1. Install `@vercel/analytics` ✅ (Already installed)
2. Import Analytics from `@vercel/analytics/react` ✅ (Already configured)
3. Add `<Analytics />` component to the root component ✅ (Already added)

The Analytics component is now properly integrated at the end of the App component's JSX return, which will track page views and user interactions once deployed to Vercel.

## Files Changed
- `frontend/src/App.tsx` - Fixed import order by moving `useEffect` import to the top

## Next Steps
To enable Web Analytics:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel using `vercel deploy`
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard under the Analytics section
3. Verify analytics are working by checking for XHR requests to `/_vercel/insights/*` in the browser's Network tab

## Notes
- The project uses npm as the package manager (package-lock.json present)
- No new dependencies were added as `@vercel/analytics` was already installed
- The implementation follows the official Vercel documentation for React applications
- Build output shows the bundle includes the analytics code (244.89 kB total JS)
